Surface area: $Bottom_{area}=side\times side=x\cdot x=x^2$. There are $4$ rectangular sides, so $Sides_{area}=4\times width\times height = 4\cdot x\cdot y$. Thus $$SA=Bottom_{area}+Sides_{area}=x^2+4xy.$$

Volume constraint: $V=Length \times Width \times Height$. Base is square so $L=W=x$, Height $=y$, and $V=256$:
\begin{align*}
V&=L\times W\times H\\
256&= x\cdot x\cdot y\\
256&=x^2y
\end{align*}

Reduce to one variable: $256=x^2y \Rightarrow y=\frac{256}{x^2}$, so
\begin{align*}
SA&=x^2+4xy\\
&=x^2+4x\left( \frac{256}{x^2}\right)\\
&=x^2+\frac{1024}{x}\\
&=x^2+1024x^{-1}
\end{align*}
Domain: $x>0$ or $(0,\infty)$.

Compute the derivative:
\begin{align*}
SA(x)&=x^2+1024x^{-1}\\
SA'(x)&=2x-1024x^{-2}=2x-\frac{1024}{x^2}
\end{align*}
Set $SA'(x)=0$:
\begin{align*}
0&=2x-\frac{1024}{x^2}\\
\frac{1024}{x^2}&=2x\\
\frac{1024}{2}&=x^3\\
512&=x^3\\
\sqrt[3]{512}&=x\\
8&=x
\end{align*}
$SA'(x)$ DNE when $x=0$ (division by $0$), but $x=0$ is not in the domain $(0,\infty)$. Thus the only critical number in the domain is $x=8$.

\textit{Sign line for $SA'(x)$:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Choose $x=1\in(0,8)$: $SA'(1)=2(1)-\frac{1024}{1^2}=-1022<0$.
\item Choose $x=10\in(8,\infty)$: $SA'(10)=2(10)-\frac{1024}{10^2}=9.76>0$.
\end{itemize}
$SA'$ goes $(-)\to(+)$ at $x=8$, so $SA$ has a local minimum at $x=8$. Since $x=8$ is the only critical number in the domain and $SA'$ changes sign there, the First Derivative Test for Absolute Extrema gives an absolute minimum at $x=8$.

