A reasonable domain would be to require $x$ be positive since it represents the length of a side. Domain: $x>0$ or $(0,\infty)$.

\begin{align*}
P'(x)&=10-\frac{2\cdot 4x}{100}-0\\
&=10-\frac{8x}{100}
\end{align*}
We check for critical numbers by determining when $P'(x)=0$:
\begin{align*}
0&=10-\frac{8x}{100}\\
\frac{8x}{100}&=10\\
8x&=1000\\
x&=\frac{1000}{8}=125
\end{align*}
$P'(x)=10-\frac{8x}{100}$ is continuous for all $x$, so $P'$ never DNE. The only critical number in our domain is $x=125$.

There are two ways to check whether $x=125$ is the location of an Absolute Maximum.

\textbf{Approach 1 (Closed Interval Method).} Since the domain is the closed interval $[0,200]$, evaluate $P(x)$ at the endpoints and the critical number:
\begin{itemize}
\item $P(0)=10\cdot 0-\frac{4\cdot 0^2}{100}-400=-400$
\item $P(125)=10\cdot 125-\frac{4\cdot (125)^2}{100}-400=225$
\item $P(200)=10\cdot 200-\frac{4\cdot (200)^2}{100}-400=0$
\end{itemize}
So we have an Absolute Maximum at $x=125$ units.

\textbf{Approach 2 (First Derivative Test for Absolute Extrema).} Build a sign line for $P'$:
\begin{itemize}
\item Choose $x=0\in[0,125)$: $P'(0)=10-\frac{8\cdot 0}{100}=10>0$.
\item Choose $x=200\in(125,200]$: $P'(200)=10-\frac{8\cdot 200}{100}=10-16=-6<0$.
\end{itemize}
$P'$ goes $(+)\to(-)$ at $x=125$, so $P$ has a local maximum at $x=125$. Since $x=125$ is the only critical number in the domain and $P'$ changes sign, the local maximum is the absolute maximum. Selling $x=125$ units maximizes profit.

\textit{Quantity to optimize:} minimize total cost of fencing.

\textit{Variables:} $x$ = width of rectangular pen, $y$ = length of rectangular pen.

\textit{Cost function:}
$$Cost_{Total}=2\cdot Cost_{side}+ Cost_{top}+Cost_{bottom} +Cost_{interior}.$$
\begin{itemize}
\item One side (length $y$) at $\$2$/ft: $Cost_{side}=2y$; two of them gives $2\cdot Cost_{side}=4y$.
\item Top and bottom (width $x$) at $\$2$/ft: $Cost_{top}=2x$, $Cost_{bottom}=2x$.
\item Interior (width $x$) at $\$1$/ft: $Cost_{interior}=x$.
\end{itemize}
Thus
\begin{align*}
Cost_{Total}&=2\cdot(2y)+2x+2x+x\\
&=4y+5x
\end{align*}

\textit{Constraint:} Area $=x\cdot y=500$, so $y=\frac{500}{x}$.

\textit{Reduce to one variable:}
\begin{align*}
Cost_{Total}&=4y+5x\\
&=4\left(\frac{500}{x}\right)+5x\\
C(x)&=\frac{2000}{x}+5x
\end{align*}

\textit{Domain:} Width is positive, so $x>0$ or $(0,\infty)$.

\begin{align*}
C(x)&=\frac{2000}{x}+5x=2000x^{-1}+5x\\
C'(x)&=-2000x^{-2}+5=-\frac{2000}{x^2}+5\\
0&=-\frac{2000}{x^2}+5\\
\frac{2000}{x^2}&=5\\
x^2&=400\\
x&=\pm 20
\end{align*}
$C'(x)$ DNE at $x=0$ (division by $0$). Critical numbers: $x=-20,\;0,\;20$. Domain is $(0,\infty)$, so the only critical number in the domain is $x=20$.

\textit{Sign line for $C'(x)$:}
\begin{itemize}
\item Choose $x=1\in(0,20)$: $C'(1)=-\frac{2000}{1^2}+5=-1995<0$.
\item Choose $x=100\in(20,\infty)$: $C'(100)=-\frac{2000}{(100)^2}+5=-0.2+5=4.8>0$.
\end{itemize}
$C'$ goes $(-)\to(+)$ at $x=20$, so $C$ has a local minimum there. Since $x=20$ is the only critical number in the domain and $C'$ changes sign, the First Derivative Test for Absolute Extrema gives an absolute minimum at $x=20$.

