Profit $=$ Revenue $-$ Cost. Cost includes fixed and variable costs:
$$C(x)=V(x)+20{,}000=0.000001x^3-0.01x^2+50x+20{,}000.$$
Profit:
\begin{align*}
P(x)&=R(x)-C(x)\\
&=-0.02x^2+150x-\left(0.000001x^3-0.01x^2+50x+20{,}000\right)\\
&=-0.000001x^3-0.01x^2+100x-20{,}000\\
P'(x)&=-0.000003x^2-0.02x+100
\end{align*}
$P'(x)$ is a quadratic, so it is defined for all $x$; $P'(x)$ never DNE. Set $P'(x)=0$:
\begin{align*}
-0.000003x^2-0.02x+100&=0\\
x&=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a},\quad a=-0.000003,\ b=-0.02,\ c=100\\
x&=\frac{-(-0.02)\pm\sqrt{(-0.02)^2-4(-0.000003)(100)}}{2(-0.000003)}\\
x&=-10{,}000\ \text{or}\ 3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3}
\end{align*}
$x=-10{,}000$ is not in $[0,7500]$, so the only critical number in the domain is $x=3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3}$.

\textbf{Approach 1 (Closed Interval Method).}
\begin{itemize}
\item $P(0)=-20{,}000$
\item $P(3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3})=165{,}185$
\item $P(7500)=-254{,}375$
\end{itemize}
Absolute Max occurs at $x=3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3}$ with profit $\approx\$165{,}185$.

\textbf{Approach 2 (First Derivative Test for Absolute Extrema).}
\begin{itemize}
\item Choose $x=0$ (left of $3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3}$): $P'(0)=100>0$.
\item Choose $x=4{,}000$ (right of $3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3}$): $P'(4000)=-28<0$.
\end{itemize}
$P'$ goes $(+)\to(-)$ at $x=3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3}$, so $P$ has a local maximum there. Since this is the only critical number in the domain where $P'$ changes sign, the First Derivative Test for Absolute Extrema gives an absolute maximum at $x=3{,}333\tfrac{1}{3}$.
